WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.560
So think about the last time you got behind the wheel of a car.

00:03.860 --> 00:04.540
Oh boy, yeah.

00:04.840 --> 00:05.060
Right.

00:05.620 --> 00:13.080
If you've been driving for years, I'm willing to bet you've had that slightly terrifying experience where you arrive at your destination, right?

00:13.280 --> 00:14.540
And you put the car in park, yeah.

00:14.740 --> 00:20.940
Yeah, you put the car in park and you suddenly realize you have absolutely no memory of the last five miles of your commute.

00:21.160 --> 00:22.060
It's like you blacked out.

00:22.300 --> 00:22.680
Exactly.

00:23.400 --> 00:25.560
You didn't actively think about the steering wheel.

00:25.820 --> 00:29.380
You didn't, you know, consciously analyze the pressure you were applying to the gas pedal.

00:29.380 --> 00:31.100
Or reciting the rules of the road.

00:31.520 --> 00:32.320
Definitely not.

00:32.740 --> 00:34.620
You were just, well, you were just driving.

00:35.000 --> 00:39.180
You were probably listening to music or maybe running through your grocery list in your head.

00:39.280 --> 00:39.540
Right.

00:39.720 --> 00:41.020
Your mind was completely elsewhere.

00:41.480 --> 00:41.680
Yeah.

00:42.080 --> 00:46.700
But now, contrast that with the very first time you ever sat in the driver's seat as a teenager.

00:46.940 --> 00:49.720
Oh, that is a complete sensory overload.

00:49.920 --> 00:50.720
Complete overload.

00:51.400 --> 00:53.220
You are gripping the wheel at 10 and 2.

00:53.660 --> 00:55.180
Your knuckles are completely white.

00:55.300 --> 00:58.320
You're staring at the speedometer like it's a ticking bomb, right?

00:58.480 --> 00:58.960
Yeah.

00:59.380 --> 01:06.620
You're consciously trying to remember which pedal is the brake and which is the gas, while simultaneously trying to check, what, three different mirrors.

01:06.780 --> 01:07.500
It's exhausting.

01:07.800 --> 01:08.500
It really is.

01:08.860 --> 01:13.300
The sheer amount of effort it takes just to keep the vehicle between two painted lines is draining.

01:13.300 --> 01:23.420
But somehow, over time, that grueling, mentally taxing ordeal morphs into an action so effortless that you can do it while practically daydreaming.

01:23.420 --> 01:32.740
And that transition, you know, from agonizingly hard to completely automatic is something you, listening right now, have experienced hundreds of times.

01:32.840 --> 01:37.500
With driving, with learning an instrument, with mastering a new piece of software at work.

01:37.540 --> 01:37.980
It's everywhere.

01:37.980 --> 01:42.040
It's arguably one of the most defining characteristics of human learning.

01:42.040 --> 02:12.040


02:12.040 --> 02:17.740
It was popularized massively by people like Daniel Kahneman in Thinking Fast and Slow.

02:17.940 --> 02:19.260
Massively is an understatement.

02:19.700 --> 02:20.280
It's everywhere.

02:20.280 --> 02:20.960
Yeah.

02:20.960 --> 02:22.940
And the narrative is incredibly sticky.

02:23.540 --> 02:26.840
We are told we have two distinct modes of thought, right?

02:27.360 --> 02:30.460
System one is the fast, intuitive, effortless brain.

02:30.600 --> 02:32.540
It's the autopilot that drives you home.

02:32.680 --> 02:33.020
Exactly.

02:33.020 --> 02:39.620
But we're also told it's prone to bias, it's emotional, and it's fundamentally a bit, well, sloppy.

02:40.440 --> 02:44.680
And then on the other side, you have system two, the hero of rationality.

02:44.900 --> 02:45.320
Right.

02:45.480 --> 02:50.140
System two is supposed to be the slow, deliberative, effortful, conscious brain.

02:50.860 --> 02:56.340
The one that steps in to do math or parallel park in a tight spot or write a complex email.

02:56.820 --> 02:57.040
Yeah.

02:57.100 --> 03:00.340
And this framework hasn't just stayed in psychology labs.

03:00.340 --> 03:04.660
It has driven public policy, management strategies, and right now, I mean,

03:04.700 --> 03:09.020
it is completely dominating how the tech industry talks about artificial intelligence.

03:09.300 --> 03:09.840
Oh, absolutely.

03:10.260 --> 03:14.620
We constantly hear debates about whether AI has achieved system two reasoning.

03:14.880 --> 03:21.360
But the material we are diving into today poses a very uncomfortable question for all of those fields.

03:21.540 --> 03:21.920
Which is?

03:22.500 --> 03:30.140
What if that entire paradigm, the very foundation of how we categorize human thought, is completely wrong?

03:30.340 --> 03:34.640
What if the two systems idea is just a complete myth?

03:34.860 --> 03:37.600
I am so excited to get into this.

03:37.980 --> 03:45.120
Because the source material we are looking at today completely rewired how I view my own habits.

03:45.380 --> 03:46.260
It's pretty mind-blowing.

03:46.460 --> 03:46.960
It really is.

03:46.960 --> 03:57.240
We are unpacking a dense, highly controversial, and frankly brilliant 2026 monograph by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

03:57.820 --> 04:03.540
It's titled, Relegated Structure, Practice, Attention, and the Dual Processed Partition.

04:03.760 --> 04:05.220
That's a mouthful of a title.

04:05.420 --> 04:05.760
It is.

04:06.040 --> 04:06.240
Yeah.

04:06.240 --> 04:17.220
But our mission today is to explore Flyxion's incredibly bold claim, which is that the two-system partition is a fundamental misinterpretation of how human practice actually works.

04:17.960 --> 04:25.300
And we are going to look at how clinging to this myth is severely distorting how we measure and understand the intelligence of AI systems today.

04:25.300 --> 04:30.020
So it's important to clarify up front what Flyxion is actually attacking here, right?

04:30.440 --> 04:37.240
The monograph isn't just suggesting that System 1 and System 2 need better names or that the boundaries between them are a bit fuzzy.

04:37.700 --> 04:38.500
No, not at all.

04:38.840 --> 04:47.140
Flyxion is arguing that treating the human mind as if it has two distinct cognitive engines is a massive, unwarranted leap of logic.

04:47.300 --> 04:48.220
A complete misread.

04:48.220 --> 04:48.620
Right.

04:49.120 --> 05:03.020
The claim is that we took a simple observation about how human performance varies, like sometimes we are fast, sometimes we are slow, and we mistakenly allowed that observation to harden into a biological claim about the architecture of the brain.

05:03.320 --> 05:11.480
So if I'm understanding the historical context Flyxion lays out in the first chapter, we really have to go back to see where the train left the tracks.

05:11.940 --> 05:15.120
Because this didn't start with condiment or behavioral economics.

05:15.140 --> 05:16.560
No, it goes way further back.

05:16.560 --> 05:19.520
The seeds were planted way back with William James, right?

05:19.660 --> 05:21.080
Yes, in the late 19th century.

05:21.320 --> 05:24.820
William James is often considered the father of American psychology.

05:25.660 --> 05:29.720
And James spent a lot of time observing and writing about the nature of habit.

05:30.000 --> 05:30.280
Okay.

05:30.780 --> 05:36.940
He vividly described the subjective internal experience of how a task changes as we practice it.

05:37.580 --> 05:44.060
He noted that as we repeat a sequence of actions, it becomes more automatic, and the feeling of conscious effort fades.

05:44.260 --> 05:44.620
Right.

05:44.620 --> 05:49.440
And this, James noted, frees up our conscious attention to focus on other things.

05:49.780 --> 05:52.360
Which is just a brilliant observation of what we all feel.

05:52.680 --> 05:53.160
It is.

05:53.380 --> 05:56.020
But here is the crucial distinction, and this is key.

05:56.700 --> 05:58.960
James was describing a subjective experience.

05:58.960 --> 06:02.300
He was talking about how it feels to perform a habit.

06:02.980 --> 06:04.520
He wasn't talking about brain anatomy.

06:04.520 --> 06:05.440
Exactly.

06:05.560 --> 06:13.140
He was absolutely not claiming that the brain is architecturally divided into a habit mechanism and a completely separate effort mechanism.

06:13.520 --> 06:16.260
You were just saying, hey, things get easier the more you do them.

06:16.780 --> 06:23.800
So how did we get from William James describing a feeling to modern science declaring we have two separate operating systems?

06:23.800 --> 06:30.320
Well, the shift began in the mid-20th century with experimental psychologists, researchers like Michael Posner and Charles Snyder.

06:30.540 --> 06:34.280
They needed to study this phenomenon in a controlled laboratory setting.

06:34.400 --> 06:35.020
Which makes sense.

06:35.260 --> 06:35.440
Right.

06:35.580 --> 06:38.020
But to do that, you need measurable variables.

06:38.120 --> 06:40.880
You can't just measure a feeling of ease, you know.

06:40.880 --> 06:43.960
So they created operational definitions.

06:44.500 --> 06:45.480
Okay, what were those definitions?

06:46.140 --> 06:55.580
They defined an automatic process as one that is executed quickly, places a very low load on working memory, and is difficult to consciously interrupt.

06:55.940 --> 06:57.280
Got it. Fast and unstoppable.

06:57.620 --> 06:57.780
Yeah.

06:58.360 --> 07:05.520
And conversely, a controlled process was defined as slow, resource-heavy, and heavily dependent on active attention.

07:05.800 --> 07:08.660
That makes total sense from an experimental design perspective.

07:08.660 --> 07:12.880
If I'm running a lab, I need to look at a stopwatch and a brain scan.

07:13.300 --> 07:14.600
I can measure speed.

07:15.080 --> 07:21.420
I can measure if a subject's working memory is overloaded by, say, giving them a secondary task.

07:21.680 --> 07:24.640
Like remembering a string of numbers while they solve a puzzle.

07:24.820 --> 07:25.100
Exactly.

07:25.780 --> 07:30.120
And for decades, those definitions were incredibly useful for designing experiments.

07:30.600 --> 07:36.420
Like, if you look at the famous Stroop test, where you are shown the word blue, but it's printed in red ink,

07:36.420 --> 07:38.500
and you have to name the color of the ink.

07:38.700 --> 07:40.300
That test is so frustrating.

07:40.560 --> 07:48.340
It is, because reading the word is fast and automatic, and it interferes with the slower, controlled process of naming the ink color.

07:48.720 --> 07:52.540
That's a classic demonstration of these operational definitions at work.

07:52.980 --> 07:53.520
It is.

07:53.960 --> 07:58.660
But even Posner and Snyder and the literature that followed routinely noted that these features,

07:58.660 --> 08:05.700
speed, low effort, uncontrollability, they do not always strictly travel together in the real world.

08:05.860 --> 08:06.120
Really?

08:06.340 --> 08:06.560
Yeah.

08:06.820 --> 08:11.960
You can have a cognitive process that is incredibly fast, but also easily interruptible.

08:12.440 --> 08:19.820
So, the lab created these neat little boxes to help measure things, but the boxes were always known to be a bit leaky.

08:19.820 --> 08:21.540
They were heuristic tools.

08:21.920 --> 08:22.980
Just tools.

08:23.500 --> 08:29.100
But then, as Flyxion meticulously documents, those laboratory tools migrated out of the lab.

08:29.520 --> 08:33.320
And this is what the monograph calls the ontological drift.

08:33.880 --> 08:34.800
Ontological drift.

08:34.940 --> 08:39.260
I really want to pause on this concept, because it feels like the crux of the whole historical error.

08:39.780 --> 08:44.480
Ontology is the philosophical study of being, of what actually exists in reality, right?

08:44.480 --> 08:45.040
Yes.

08:46.040 --> 08:51.160
And Flyxion argues that over time, particularly as this research was picked up by early theorists

08:51.160 --> 08:56.420
and reasoning like Peter Wasson and Jonathan Evans, and then later heavily popularized by

08:56.420 --> 09:02.400
Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman in behavioral economics, the nuance was just lost.

09:02.580 --> 09:03.140
It evaporated.

09:03.400 --> 09:03.620
Yeah.

09:03.880 --> 09:08.800
A description of how performance varies under different conditions drifted into a concrete belief

09:08.800 --> 09:10.260
about how the mind is built.

09:10.440 --> 09:10.680
Wow.

09:10.680 --> 09:15.700
The operational contrast used to sort data points in a spreadsheet became an ontological

09:15.700 --> 09:21.340
claim about the existence of two distinct qualitative engines inside the human skull.

09:21.940 --> 09:26.360
I was trying to come up with an analogy for this while I was reading the paper, and tell

09:26.360 --> 09:26.960
me if this tracks.

09:27.920 --> 09:31.500
It feels like noticing that my physical body can do two different things, right?

09:31.680 --> 09:32.340
Okay, let's hear it.

09:32.500 --> 09:35.940
I could walk slowly down the street, which takes almost no energy.

09:36.200 --> 09:39.440
I can hold a conversation while I do it, and my heart rate stays low.

09:39.440 --> 09:45.880
Or, I can absolutely sprint as fast as I can, which requires massive physical exertion,

09:46.240 --> 09:48.340
complete focus, and I definitely can't talk while I do it.

09:48.500 --> 09:49.400
Okay, I follow you.

09:49.900 --> 09:55.420
So, if I apply this ontological drift logic, I would observe those two different performance

09:55.420 --> 10:01.120
profiles, slow and effortless versus fast and exhausting, and I would conclude that my

10:01.120 --> 10:07.120
body must therefore contain a walking engine and a completely separate anatomically distinct

10:07.120 --> 10:08.060
sprinting engine.

10:08.060 --> 10:09.980
When the reality is?

10:10.120 --> 10:15.460
When the reality is I only have one musculoskeletal system, it's just operating at different levels

10:15.460 --> 10:19.420
of intensity, drawing on different metabolic pathways, depending on the demand.

10:19.540 --> 10:20.520
I don't have two bodies.

10:20.960 --> 10:26.880
That is a remarkably precise analogy for what Flyxion is arguing happened in cognitive science.

10:27.280 --> 10:29.020
We have one cognitive capacity.

10:29.020 --> 10:34.900
It can operate at a low, highly compressed intensity, or it can operate at a high, explicitly

10:34.900 --> 10:36.120
coordinated intensity.

10:36.420 --> 10:37.720
But there aren't two systems.

10:37.960 --> 10:38.140
Right.

10:38.620 --> 10:43.140
Concluding that there are two separate systems is like looking for the sprinting engine in

10:43.140 --> 10:44.360
your legs that doesn't exist.

10:44.920 --> 10:48.220
It's just a different mode of operation for the same underlying structure.

10:48.220 --> 10:52.660
And Flyxion points out that this drift isn't just a harmless academic misunderstanding.

10:53.280 --> 10:55.500
It has created real world baggage.

10:56.020 --> 10:59.900
One of the things that stood out to me the most in the first chapter was this idea of the

10:59.900 --> 11:02.380
moralization of effort.

11:02.380 --> 11:07.200
Oh, this is a huge consequence of the pop psychology version of dual process theory.

11:07.940 --> 11:13.080
In the popular narrative, System 1 is constantly cast in a somewhat negative light.

11:13.200 --> 11:13.900
Yeah, it's the bad guy.

11:14.060 --> 11:15.600
It's the source of our cognitive biases.

11:15.860 --> 11:16.500
It's impulsive.

11:16.740 --> 11:17.840
It relies on stereotypes.

11:17.980 --> 11:18.840
It jumps to conclusions.

11:19.420 --> 11:20.900
It is treated with suspicion.

11:21.600 --> 11:24.180
Meanwhile, System 2 is cast as the noble hero.

11:24.600 --> 11:25.340
It is rational.

11:25.540 --> 11:27.060
It corrects the errors of System 1.

11:27.380 --> 11:29.040
It is conscientious and logical.

11:29.500 --> 11:29.740
Right.

11:29.740 --> 11:33.200
And if you want to be a smart, rational person, you're supposed to activate System 2.

11:33.700 --> 11:37.180
Which means, culturally, we have begun to moralize effort.

11:37.740 --> 11:41.020
We assume that if a decision feels slow and difficult, it must be more rational.

11:41.280 --> 11:44.880
And if a decision is fast and effortless, it is inherently suspicious.

11:45.120 --> 11:48.440
But that completely falls apart when you look at true human expertise.

11:49.100 --> 11:50.600
Let's take a chess grandmaster.

11:50.800 --> 11:51.240
Great example.

11:51.980 --> 11:55.840
A grandmaster can look at a board configuration they have never seen before in their life,

11:56.100 --> 11:59.640
and in literally half a second, they can intuit the strongest move.

12:00.420 --> 12:02.020
It is lightning fast.

12:02.120 --> 12:03.840
It requires zero conscious effort.

12:04.100 --> 12:04.200
Yep.

12:04.600 --> 12:09.540
By the strict definitions of dual process theory, that grandmaster is using System 1.

12:10.320 --> 12:15.220
And therefore, according to the pop culture narrative, we should be suspicious of that move.

12:15.740 --> 12:17.500
Because it's impulsive and biased.

12:17.500 --> 12:24.440
But we know objectively that the grandmaster's move is a display of peak rationality and profound structural understanding.

12:24.960 --> 12:25.240
Exactly.

12:25.940 --> 12:28.540
The free work awkwardly penalizes mastery.

12:29.360 --> 12:35.100
It forces us into this bizarre corner where an incredibly effortful, slow judgment made by a novice,

12:35.440 --> 12:38.060
who is, you know, struggling to remember how the knight moves,

12:38.620 --> 12:44.900
is classified as System 2, and therefore supposedly more rational than the instant, effortless brilliance of the master.

12:44.900 --> 12:45.840
It's backwards.

12:46.480 --> 12:47.140
It makes no sense.

12:47.660 --> 12:50.460
The two-system vocabulary just ties itself in knots,

12:50.780 --> 12:54.560
trying to explain how an expert can be both fast and rational at the same time.

12:54.940 --> 13:01.000
Now, in fairness to the field, the more careful practitioners of dual process theory are aware of these contradictions.

13:01.720 --> 13:04.060
Researchers like Jonathan Evans and Keith Stanovich,

13:04.120 --> 13:07.200
who are really the heavy hitters in defining the modern version of this theory,

13:07.580 --> 13:12.320
they actually advocate against using the terms System 1 and System 2 for exactly this reason.

13:12.320 --> 13:13.060
Oh, they do?

13:13.060 --> 13:16.140
Yeah, they prefer Type 1 and Type 2 processing,

13:16.700 --> 13:21.500
precisely to avoid the implication that there are two literal, localized machines in the brain.

13:21.720 --> 13:22.000
Okay.

13:22.540 --> 13:24.320
So they recognize the naming problem.

13:24.940 --> 13:27.240
But do they still believe in the fundamental division?

13:27.480 --> 13:27.900
They do.

13:28.680 --> 13:31.980
They argue that even if speed and effort are just correlates,

13:32.400 --> 13:35.020
there is still a hard architectural dividing line.

13:35.700 --> 13:39.720
And for Evans and Stanovich, that dividing line is working memory.

13:40.100 --> 13:41.040
Working memory.

13:41.040 --> 13:43.760
Let's make sure we define that for everyone listening.

13:44.540 --> 13:48.680
Working memory is essentially the brain's temporary scratch pad, right?

13:48.820 --> 13:49.780
That's a perfect way to put it.

13:50.120 --> 13:54.100
It's the metal space where you hold information while you actively manipulate it.

13:54.400 --> 13:57.640
Like, when you're trying to calculate an 18% tip in your head,

13:57.820 --> 14:01.780
you're holding the total bill amount in your working memory while you do the math.

14:02.040 --> 14:03.000
That's a perfect definition.

14:03.400 --> 14:08.040
Evans and Stanovich argue that Type 2 processing definitively requires working memory.

14:08.040 --> 14:11.540
And specifically, it requires what they call cognitive decoupling.

14:12.060 --> 14:13.200
Cognitive decoupling.

14:13.600 --> 14:13.780
Yeah.

14:13.920 --> 14:17.660
The ability to hold a hypothetical scenario in your mind separately from reality.

14:18.120 --> 14:22.840
They argue that Type 1 processing simply does not use this working memory capacity.

14:23.560 --> 14:25.980
Therefore, they claim the division is real and structural.

14:25.980 --> 14:28.980
One type uses a scratch pad, the other doesn't.

14:29.520 --> 14:31.220
That sounds like a pretty solid defense, honestly.

14:31.540 --> 14:36.700
If one process uses a specific cognitive resource and the other completely bypasses it,

14:36.760 --> 14:38.960
that feels like a real architectural difference.

14:39.460 --> 14:43.000
How does Flyxion counter that specific working memory argument?

14:43.000 --> 14:47.760
Flyxion's counter-argument is the conceptual fulcrum of the entire monograph.

14:48.520 --> 14:51.580
A dual-process theorist looks at a novice doing a task slowly,

14:51.980 --> 14:53.320
using a lot of working memory,

14:53.480 --> 14:55.420
and then looks at an expert doing it quickly,

14:55.540 --> 14:56.820
using almost no working memory,

14:57.000 --> 14:57.760
and concludes,

14:58.220 --> 15:01.480
ah, the expert has handed the task over to a different system.

15:01.720 --> 15:01.940
Right.

15:02.580 --> 15:03.500
Flyxion says,

15:03.840 --> 15:04.100
no.

15:04.700 --> 15:06.940
The expert is using the exact same system,

15:06.940 --> 15:11.540
but practice has changed the task's working memory demands through compression.

15:12.140 --> 15:12.620
Compression?

15:13.000 --> 15:16.380
Like taking a massive video file and zipping it into a smaller format,

15:16.560 --> 15:18.480
so it takes up less space on your hard drive.

15:18.600 --> 15:19.160
Precisely.

15:19.500 --> 15:23.340
The cognitive operation itself hasn't been substituted for a blind reflex.

15:23.860 --> 15:28.100
The master chess player is still evaluating board position, space, and tempo.

15:28.420 --> 15:29.380
They're still doing the work.

15:29.560 --> 15:32.160
Yes, but those evaluations have been compressed.

15:32.640 --> 15:34.800
The same underlying capacity is operating,

15:34.800 --> 15:36.840
but because the patterns are so familiar,

15:37.300 --> 15:39.900
it can process them at a much lower resolution,

15:40.140 --> 15:42.320
which requires a drastically smaller footprint,

15:42.320 --> 15:44.280
on the working memory scratch pad.

15:44.660 --> 15:44.680
Okay.

15:44.880 --> 15:49.680
So, if the task isn't being handed off to a secret fast machine in the basement of our brain,

15:50.480 --> 15:52.880
what exactly is happening during that compression?

15:53.260 --> 15:55.880
This is what I was desperate to understand while reading.

15:56.540 --> 15:57.380
It's fascinating.

15:57.380 --> 16:00.780
If it's not a walking engine and a sprinting engine,

16:01.260 --> 16:03.960
what is the brain physically and conceptually doing

16:03.960 --> 16:07.960
when a grueling task becomes a lightning-fast reflex?

16:08.480 --> 16:11.300
This brings us to the beating heart of Flyxion's thesis,

16:12.000 --> 16:13.340
aspect relegation theory.

16:13.780 --> 16:14.820
Aspect relegation.

16:15.480 --> 16:17.620
This is the big paradigm replacement,

16:18.020 --> 16:20.780
so let's start at the beginning of the learning process.

16:20.780 --> 16:23.140
When I'm learning something brand new,

16:23.720 --> 16:26.560
how does aspect relegation theory describe that state?

16:26.940 --> 16:28.660
When a task is entirely novel,

16:29.020 --> 16:32.160
your mind has to form what Flyxion calls an attention set.

16:32.800 --> 16:35.020
Because you don't know what matters and what doesn't,

16:35.420 --> 16:37.920
this attention set has to explicitly coordinate

16:37.920 --> 16:39.540
every single constraint,

16:39.840 --> 16:40.380
every step,

16:40.760 --> 16:42.600
and every conditional branch of the task.

16:42.720 --> 16:43.900
Operating at high resolution.

16:44.420 --> 16:46.000
Yes, incredibly high resolution.

16:46.480 --> 16:47.980
Let's use the piano as an example.

16:47.980 --> 16:50.700
Think of an adult novice sitting down

16:50.700 --> 16:52.540
for their very first piano lesson.

16:53.460 --> 16:54.860
To play even a simple scale,

16:55.320 --> 16:57.700
their attention set must explicitly track

16:57.700 --> 16:59.500
an overwhelming number of distinctions.

16:59.920 --> 17:01.280
They have to look at the sheet music

17:01.280 --> 17:04.720
and consciously translate a black dot on a specific line

17:04.720 --> 17:06.560
into the concept of the note C.

17:06.660 --> 17:06.840
Right.

17:07.320 --> 17:10.180
Then they have to consciously locate the C key

17:10.180 --> 17:11.420
on the physical board.

17:12.040 --> 17:15.260
Then they have to explicitly command their index finger,

17:15.560 --> 17:17.000
not their thumb, their index finger,

17:17.000 --> 17:18.560
to press that key.

17:19.140 --> 17:21.240
They have to monitor the posture of their wrist.

17:21.560 --> 17:23.120
They have to count the rhythm out loud.

17:23.240 --> 17:25.140
One, two, three, four.

17:25.280 --> 17:25.480
Right.

17:25.560 --> 17:26.660
And their brain is just screaming,

17:26.800 --> 17:28.000
I don't have enough bandwidth for this.

17:28.160 --> 17:28.600
Exactly.

17:28.940 --> 17:30.660
All of these distinct demands are competing

17:30.660 --> 17:32.700
for a highly limited attentional budget.

17:33.220 --> 17:34.580
That's why learning is so exhausting.

17:34.940 --> 17:37.060
It is cognitively expensive.

17:37.760 --> 17:40.160
But, as we established with William James,

17:40.680 --> 17:41.400
with repetition,

17:41.820 --> 17:42.960
something remarkable happens.

17:43.600 --> 17:45.140
The strategies begin to stabilize.

17:45.140 --> 17:45.700
Okay.

17:45.700 --> 17:47.460
The novice figures out

17:47.460 --> 17:48.640
that the spatial relationship

17:48.640 --> 17:49.920
between the keys is constant.

17:50.620 --> 17:52.040
The translation from the sheet music

17:52.040 --> 17:52.940
to the finger movement

17:52.940 --> 17:53.960
becomes less erratic.

17:54.740 --> 17:56.080
And as stability increases,

17:56.360 --> 17:57.940
the mind naturally economizes.

17:58.560 --> 18:00.760
It does this by relegating these steps.

18:01.340 --> 18:01.840
Relegating?

18:02.080 --> 18:04.520
What exactly does that word mean in this context?

18:04.840 --> 18:06.900
Because it doesn't mean deleting them, right?

18:07.280 --> 18:09.640
No, and that is the critical difference

18:09.640 --> 18:12.780
between Vlycarian and the dual process theorists.

18:12.780 --> 18:16.420
This is formalized in definition 3.1 of the text.

18:16.800 --> 18:18.780
A process is considered relegated

18:18.780 --> 18:21.120
when it continues to operate reliably,

18:21.500 --> 18:23.320
but its internal distinctions

18:23.320 --> 18:25.180
are no longer explicitly tracked

18:25.180 --> 18:26.360
by your active attention.

18:26.740 --> 18:27.000
Okay.

18:27.140 --> 18:28.220
Let me make sure I have this.

18:28.700 --> 18:30.160
The process doesn't stop.

18:30.700 --> 18:32.740
The pianist is still reading the sheet music.

18:33.300 --> 18:34.960
They're still putting their index finger

18:34.960 --> 18:35.740
on the C key.

18:36.160 --> 18:38.000
They are still holding their wrists correctly.

18:38.280 --> 18:38.600
Yes.

18:38.600 --> 18:40.580
But they aren't supervising those actions

18:40.580 --> 18:42.440
with their conscious attention anymore.

18:42.720 --> 18:43.280
Exactly.

18:43.920 --> 18:45.160
The distinctions are compressed

18:45.160 --> 18:46.440
out of active view.

18:47.020 --> 18:48.480
They are relegated to the background.

18:49.120 --> 18:50.640
The structural complexity of the task

18:50.640 --> 18:51.560
hasn't been replaced

18:51.560 --> 18:53.240
by a dumb System 1 reflex.

18:53.780 --> 18:55.420
It is the exact same structure,

18:55.660 --> 18:57.140
just running unsupervised.

18:57.440 --> 18:57.980
Wow.

18:58.460 --> 18:59.600
And because it's running unsupervised,

19:00.400 --> 19:01.580
the practice pianist

19:01.580 --> 19:03.660
suddenly has their attentional budget freed up.

19:03.660 --> 19:06.340
Now they can use their explicit attention

19:06.340 --> 19:08.200
to focus on the emotional phrasing

19:08.200 --> 19:08.740
of the music,

19:08.980 --> 19:10.440
or the dynamic volume,

19:10.660 --> 19:11.820
or even reading ahead

19:11.820 --> 19:12.720
to the next measure,

19:13.280 --> 19:14.860
things the novice couldn't even dream

19:14.860 --> 19:15.460
of attempting.

19:16.220 --> 19:17.820
So the transition from slow and clumsy

19:17.820 --> 19:19.200
to fast and fluent

19:19.200 --> 19:21.800
isn't a shift from System 2 to System 1.

19:22.160 --> 19:24.120
It is simply a shift in the resolution

19:24.120 --> 19:26.440
at which a single cognitive capacity

19:26.440 --> 19:28.060
is being exercised.

19:28.140 --> 19:28.980
That's it perfectly.

19:29.380 --> 19:30.720
The complexity gets folded up

19:30.720 --> 19:31.640
and tucked away.

19:31.960 --> 19:33.260
That is the paradigm shift.

19:33.260 --> 19:35.520
And it leads to a realization

19:35.520 --> 19:36.720
that I think many people

19:36.720 --> 19:37.520
intuitively know,

19:37.620 --> 19:38.740
but science has struggled

19:38.740 --> 19:39.400
to articulate.

19:40.400 --> 19:42.520
Ease is not simplicity.

19:43.520 --> 19:43.840
Yes.

19:44.460 --> 19:46.400
I loved this point in the reading.

19:47.020 --> 19:47.920
Just because a task

19:47.920 --> 19:49.360
feels incredibly easy

19:49.360 --> 19:50.060
to an expert

19:50.060 --> 19:52.160
does not mean the task itself

19:52.160 --> 19:53.860
has magically become simple.

19:54.180 --> 19:54.740
Not at all.

19:55.100 --> 19:56.320
The complexity hasn't vanished

19:56.320 --> 19:57.000
into thin air.

19:57.120 --> 19:57.720
It has just been

19:57.720 --> 19:59.780
internalized by the expert.

20:00.340 --> 20:01.840
The complexity is still actively

20:01.840 --> 20:03.000
operating under the surface.

20:03.260 --> 20:04.580
Maintaining the performance.

20:05.080 --> 20:07.100
Flyxion uses this phenomenal analogy

20:07.100 --> 20:07.640
in the text

20:07.640 --> 20:08.540
to illustrate this.

20:09.040 --> 20:10.220
The derelict building.

20:10.480 --> 20:12.380
It really helped me visualize it.

20:12.480 --> 20:13.420
It's a great image.

20:13.700 --> 20:15.120
So imagine you have two people

20:15.120 --> 20:15.980
standing on a sidewalk

20:15.980 --> 20:17.540
looking across the street

20:17.540 --> 20:18.920
at a collapsing,

20:19.520 --> 20:21.000
disordered, derelict building.

20:21.520 --> 20:22.840
Half the roof is caved in.

20:23.060 --> 20:23.780
Beams are exposed.

20:23.900 --> 20:24.380
It's a mess.

20:24.720 --> 20:24.960
Right.

20:24.960 --> 20:26.800
One person is a total novice

20:26.800 --> 20:28.120
who has never swung a hammer.

20:28.120 --> 20:29.540
The other person

20:29.540 --> 20:31.280
is a master structural engineer

20:31.280 --> 20:31.800
and builder.

20:32.500 --> 20:34.340
The novice looks at that building

20:34.340 --> 20:35.660
and just sees overwhelming

20:35.660 --> 20:37.340
intractable chaos.

20:37.780 --> 20:38.620
They don't even know

20:38.620 --> 20:39.740
where their eyes should focus.

20:40.380 --> 20:41.300
The visual information

20:41.300 --> 20:42.220
is just noise.

20:42.780 --> 20:43.140
Overload.

20:43.480 --> 20:43.680
Again.

20:43.780 --> 20:44.060
Exactly.

20:44.740 --> 20:45.440
But the expert

20:45.440 --> 20:47.320
looks at the exact same building

20:47.320 --> 20:49.080
and instantly sees

20:49.080 --> 20:50.220
a highly tractable

20:50.220 --> 20:51.240
sequence of operations.

20:51.940 --> 20:52.880
In a split second,

20:53.260 --> 20:54.840
they perceive the structural integrity

20:54.840 --> 20:55.920
of the remaining walls.

20:56.240 --> 20:57.160
They identify which

20:57.160 --> 20:58.760
load-bearing pillars have failed.

20:59.240 --> 21:00.500
And they subconsciously

21:00.500 --> 21:02.140
sequence out the subtasks

21:02.140 --> 21:03.900
required to shore up the roof.

21:04.400 --> 21:05.220
And what is vital

21:05.220 --> 21:06.100
to understand here

21:06.100 --> 21:07.640
and what aspect

21:07.640 --> 21:08.840
relegation theory highlights

21:08.840 --> 21:10.860
is that the building itself

21:10.860 --> 21:11.740
has not changed.

21:12.160 --> 21:13.180
The photons entering

21:13.180 --> 21:14.180
the retinas of both

21:14.180 --> 21:15.320
the novice and the expert

21:15.320 --> 21:15.940
are identical.

21:16.240 --> 21:16.460
Right.

21:16.460 --> 21:17.800
The external reality

21:17.800 --> 21:19.240
is exactly the same.

21:19.680 --> 21:20.680
What has changed

21:20.680 --> 21:22.100
is the expert's

21:22.100 --> 21:23.280
internalized structure.

21:24.080 --> 21:24.740
The expert has been

21:24.740 --> 21:26.280
years stabilizing

21:26.280 --> 21:27.280
decompositions

21:27.280 --> 21:28.620
for this specific

21:28.620 --> 21:29.420
class of problem.

21:29.820 --> 21:30.060
Yes.

21:30.260 --> 21:31.180
The task of assessing

21:31.180 --> 21:31.660
the building

21:31.660 --> 21:32.480
is easy for them.

21:33.040 --> 21:33.920
It feels like a

21:33.920 --> 21:34.640
fast,

21:34.860 --> 21:36.020
system-one intuition.

21:36.320 --> 21:37.220
Not because the building

21:37.220 --> 21:37.760
is simple,

21:38.320 --> 21:39.220
but because the expert

21:39.220 --> 21:39.900
has relegated

21:39.900 --> 21:41.040
all those complex

21:41.040 --> 21:41.960
structural assessments.

21:42.400 --> 21:43.180
And this brings up

21:43.180 --> 21:44.360
a feature of relegated

21:44.360 --> 21:45.080
structure that,

21:45.240 --> 21:45.680
in my view,

21:45.780 --> 21:46.800
completely shatters

21:46.800 --> 21:48.460
the old dual-process framework.

21:49.080 --> 21:49.660
The principle

21:49.660 --> 21:50.860
of reversibility.

21:51.560 --> 21:52.080
Reversibility.

21:52.640 --> 21:53.640
If relegation

21:53.640 --> 21:54.640
is just compression,

21:54.740 --> 21:56.080
it means it can be

21:56.080 --> 21:57.240
uncompressed.

21:57.680 --> 21:58.100
Precisely.

21:58.420 --> 21:59.580
If a relegated routine

21:59.580 --> 22:00.420
is still actively

22:00.420 --> 22:01.260
running in the background,

22:01.580 --> 22:02.320
just untracked

22:02.320 --> 22:03.260
by explicit attention,

22:03.460 --> 22:04.300
it means that if

22:04.300 --> 22:05.100
something goes wrong,

22:05.160 --> 22:05.800
it can be promoted

22:05.800 --> 22:07.120
back into explicit

22:07.120 --> 22:08.420
high-resolution view.

22:08.420 --> 22:10.200
Let's go back to my

22:10.200 --> 22:11.260
driving commute analogy

22:11.260 --> 22:11.960
from the beginning.

22:12.640 --> 22:13.220
I'm driving my

22:13.220 --> 22:13.920
familiar route,

22:14.100 --> 22:15.400
I'm completely zoned out,

22:15.640 --> 22:16.460
my driving behavior

22:16.460 --> 22:18.220
is deeply relegated.

22:18.580 --> 22:19.640
I'm not consciously

22:19.640 --> 22:20.580
tracking anything.

22:21.160 --> 22:22.220
According to dual-process

22:22.220 --> 22:22.580
theory,

22:22.660 --> 22:23.780
I'm fully in system one.

22:23.980 --> 22:24.240
Right.

22:24.600 --> 22:25.200
But suddenly,

22:25.440 --> 22:26.400
I come over a hill,

22:26.800 --> 22:27.400
and there is a

22:27.400 --> 22:28.600
massive detour sign,

22:28.920 --> 22:29.880
orange cones everywhere,

22:30.040 --> 22:31.040
and construction equipment

22:31.040 --> 22:32.000
blocking all three lanes.

22:32.100 --> 22:33.020
A total disruption.

22:33.020 --> 22:34.440
If I were truly

22:34.440 --> 22:35.340
operating on a

22:35.340 --> 22:35.680
blind,

22:35.980 --> 22:37.000
dumb reflex system,

22:37.160 --> 22:37.900
I would just crash

22:37.900 --> 22:38.740
into the barricade.

22:39.160 --> 22:39.900
But I don't.

22:40.040 --> 22:40.980
No, you don't.

22:41.060 --> 22:41.440
Because your

22:41.440 --> 22:42.620
environmental expectations

22:42.620 --> 22:43.000
failed.

22:43.700 --> 22:44.340
The environment

22:44.340 --> 22:45.400
suddenly became

22:45.400 --> 22:45.980
unpredictable.

22:46.480 --> 22:47.560
Your background monitor

22:47.560 --> 22:48.840
detected a mismatch,

22:48.900 --> 22:49.600
and instantly,

22:50.060 --> 22:51.160
your relegated navigation

22:51.160 --> 22:52.120
structure is promoted

22:52.120 --> 22:53.180
back to high resolution.

22:53.360 --> 22:54.240
I wake up, basically.

22:54.440 --> 22:55.440
You don't switch

22:55.440 --> 22:56.400
on a new brain.

22:56.740 --> 22:57.380
You just widen

22:57.380 --> 22:57.980
the aperture

22:57.980 --> 22:58.720
of your attention.

22:59.200 --> 23:00.040
You suddenly start

23:00.040 --> 23:01.040
explicitly tracking

23:01.040 --> 23:02.020
the street signs again.

23:02.020 --> 23:03.080
You consciously

23:03.080 --> 23:04.580
analyze alternate routes.

23:05.000 --> 23:06.020
You actively watch

23:06.020 --> 23:06.900
the braking patterns

23:06.900 --> 23:07.860
of the cars around you.

23:08.200 --> 23:09.520
The exact same operations

23:09.520 --> 23:10.080
that were running

23:10.080 --> 23:11.220
silently in the background

23:11.220 --> 23:12.280
just stepped back

23:12.280 --> 23:12.880
into the foreground.

23:13.620 --> 23:14.240
I didn't switch

23:14.240 --> 23:15.400
from a fast brain

23:15.400 --> 23:16.480
to a slow brain.

23:17.020 --> 23:17.680
I just changed

23:17.680 --> 23:18.820
the resolution of my focus.

23:19.240 --> 23:19.580
Exactly.

23:20.180 --> 23:21.780
And if we accept this,

23:22.100 --> 23:23.160
that practice changes

23:23.160 --> 23:24.080
our internal structure

23:24.080 --> 23:24.820
and the resolution

23:24.820 --> 23:25.480
of our attention,

23:25.960 --> 23:27.460
it leads to what I think

23:27.460 --> 23:28.620
is the most mind-bending

23:28.620 --> 23:29.840
part of this entire monograph.

23:30.720 --> 23:31.820
It changes the

23:31.820 --> 23:33.100
mathematical and logical

23:33.100 --> 23:34.020
reality of what

23:34.020 --> 23:35.800
difficulty actually is.

23:36.200 --> 23:36.900
You're referring to

23:36.900 --> 23:37.720
the reversal argument

23:37.720 --> 23:38.380
in Chapter 4?

23:38.800 --> 23:39.980
Yes, and I'm going

23:39.980 --> 23:40.760
to be completely honest

23:40.760 --> 23:41.060
with you.

23:41.400 --> 23:42.380
When I first read

23:42.380 --> 23:42.940
Chapter 4,

23:43.160 --> 23:44.000
I had to read it

23:44.000 --> 23:44.540
three times.

23:45.060 --> 23:45.700
It's dense.

23:46.140 --> 23:46.560
It is.

23:47.560 --> 23:48.460
Flyxion is presenting

23:48.460 --> 23:49.680
a formal mathematical

23:49.680 --> 23:50.680
proof demonstrating

23:50.680 --> 23:51.840
that there is no such

23:51.840 --> 23:53.560
thing as an intrinsically

23:53.560 --> 23:55.060
fast or intrinsically

23:55.060 --> 23:55.960
slow task.

23:56.420 --> 23:57.440
And my initial reaction

23:57.440 --> 23:58.700
was, that can't be true.

23:58.700 --> 24:00.720
It's deeply counterintuitive

24:00.720 --> 24:01.720
because we are used

24:01.720 --> 24:02.880
to categorizing things

24:02.880 --> 24:03.460
by difficulty.

24:03.720 --> 24:03.920
Right.

24:04.160 --> 24:04.820
I think of myself,

24:05.060 --> 24:05.880
basic addition,

24:06.320 --> 24:06.880
2 plus 2,

24:07.300 --> 24:08.800
is intrinsically easy.

24:09.700 --> 24:10.420
Solving a differential

24:10.420 --> 24:11.340
calculus equation

24:11.340 --> 24:12.660
is intrinsically hard.

24:13.440 --> 24:14.240
How can a researcher

24:14.240 --> 24:15.260
claim that tasks

24:15.260 --> 24:17.040
don't have intrinsic difficulty?

24:17.400 --> 24:18.540
It helps to separate

24:18.540 --> 24:20.140
the complexity of a task

24:20.140 --> 24:21.780
from the cognitive difficulty

24:21.780 --> 24:23.040
a specific person

24:23.040 --> 24:23.920
experiences when

24:23.920 --> 24:24.560
performing it.

24:25.160 --> 24:26.000
Flyxion isn't saying

24:26.000 --> 24:27.700
calculus isn't complex.

24:27.700 --> 24:29.280
Okay, good.

24:29.420 --> 24:29.940
Because it is.

24:30.120 --> 24:30.780
Very much so.

24:31.020 --> 24:32.180
The argument is that

24:32.180 --> 24:33.560
you cannot partition tasks

24:33.560 --> 24:34.400
in the real world

24:34.400 --> 24:36.100
into system 1 tasks

24:36.100 --> 24:37.180
and system 2 tasks

24:37.180 --> 24:38.360
based on how fast

24:38.360 --> 24:39.060
or effortless

24:39.060 --> 24:39.880
they are to perform.

24:40.260 --> 24:41.620
Because speed and effort

24:41.620 --> 24:42.360
are not properties

24:42.360 --> 24:43.320
of the task,

24:43.840 --> 24:44.500
they are properties

24:44.500 --> 24:45.320
of the relationship

24:45.320 --> 24:46.100
between the task

24:46.100 --> 24:46.640
and the agent.

24:47.120 --> 24:48.420
Okay, walk me through

24:48.420 --> 24:49.560
the keypad example

24:49.560 --> 24:50.280
from the text.

24:50.520 --> 24:51.380
Because that was the moment

24:51.380 --> 24:52.380
it finally clicked for me.

24:52.380 --> 24:53.880
It's an elegant illustration.

24:54.720 --> 24:55.480
Picture a standard

24:55.480 --> 24:56.380
telephone keypad.

24:56.380 --> 24:58.220
Whether it's a smartphone screen

24:58.220 --> 24:59.660
or an old landline,

25:00.160 --> 25:01.120
the numbers 1, 2, and 3

25:01.120 --> 25:02.280
are always on the top row,

25:02.580 --> 25:03.640
4, 5, 6 in the middle.

25:03.800 --> 25:04.800
Yep, very familiar.

25:05.100 --> 25:06.240
Now imagine asking

25:06.240 --> 25:07.060
an average person

25:07.060 --> 25:08.020
to enter a specific

25:08.020 --> 25:08.900
four-digit PIN,

25:09.040 --> 25:11.280
say 1, 5, 9, 3,

25:11.600 --> 25:12.600
on that telephone keypad.

25:12.920 --> 25:14.500
For almost anyone today,

25:14.620 --> 25:16.060
that feels completely effortless.

25:16.200 --> 25:16.660
It's automatic.

25:16.860 --> 25:18.420
It's deeply relegated.

25:18.580 --> 25:19.400
It takes half a second.

25:19.720 --> 25:20.780
Now picture a standard

25:20.780 --> 25:21.860
desktop calculator

25:21.860 --> 25:23.340
or the 10-key number pad

25:23.340 --> 25:24.020
on the right side

25:24.020 --> 25:24.880
of a computer keyboard.

25:24.880 --> 25:26.840
On a calculator layout,

25:26.940 --> 25:28.200
the numbers 7, 8, and 9

25:28.200 --> 25:29.060
are on the top row.

25:29.480 --> 25:30.360
The 1, 2, and 3

25:30.360 --> 25:30.980
are on the bottom.

25:31.240 --> 25:32.000
Right, it's flipped.

25:32.520 --> 25:33.980
Ask that exact same person

25:33.980 --> 25:35.360
to enter the exact same

25:35.360 --> 25:36.100
four-digit PIN,

25:36.340 --> 25:37.540
1, 5, 9, 3,

25:37.700 --> 25:39.280
on the calculator layout.

25:39.540 --> 25:40.680
It feels incredibly slow.

25:41.240 --> 25:41.780
It's clumsy.

25:42.260 --> 25:43.540
You have to consciously stop,

25:43.660 --> 25:44.340
scan the pad,

25:44.480 --> 25:45.060
find the 1,

25:45.180 --> 25:45.780
move your finger,

25:45.900 --> 25:46.440
find the 5.

25:46.620 --> 25:47.660
It feels effortful.

25:48.160 --> 25:49.240
Now think about the implications

25:49.240 --> 25:50.120
of that observation.

25:50.740 --> 25:51.760
The objective task

25:51.760 --> 25:52.480
is identical.

25:52.480 --> 25:53.520
You are pressing

25:53.520 --> 25:54.860
four specific digits

25:54.860 --> 25:56.060
in a specific sequence.

25:56.380 --> 25:57.720
The informational content

25:57.720 --> 25:58.360
of the task

25:58.360 --> 25:59.260
has not changed

25:59.260 --> 26:00.200
one iota.

26:00.780 --> 26:01.800
But the cognitive cost,

26:02.180 --> 26:02.820
the difficulty,

26:03.200 --> 26:03.780
the speed,

26:04.180 --> 26:05.200
is completely reversed.

26:05.900 --> 26:06.420
Exactly.

26:06.860 --> 26:07.580
The only difference

26:07.580 --> 26:08.740
is the practice history

26:08.740 --> 26:09.400
of the person

26:09.400 --> 26:10.400
pressing the buttons.

26:11.200 --> 26:11.800
Modern humans

26:11.800 --> 26:13.200
spend thousands of hours

26:13.200 --> 26:13.840
interacting with

